 needed to make an airline run but  you know suddenly a lot of those  potential employees have a lot of other  options meanwhile the added burden of  extra shifts and longer hours for the  workers companies do have can lead to  higher rates of staff burnout and sick  leave so you know for the employees who  are there you know some of them are  complaining about  new levels of strain especially over the  summer and the last couple of months  airlines like at southwest have been  saying that they're having problems with  higher rates of sick leave and  absenteeism and some of that might be  due to covid  as a result worker unions have become  more vocal about another issue  disrupting air travel scheduling  mismanagement typical hiccups that may  have normally delayed a flight for one  day turn into multi-day meltdowns caused  by staff scheduling holes consider this  domino effect over optimistic flight  scheduling in the midst of a staffing  shortage may lead to employees being  overworked creating higher levels of  staff burnout and sick leave and leading  to even more scheduling issues and  cancelled flights pilots have said you  know maybe they're a little bit  reluctant  to pick up extra trips you know when  there's an option to pick up some  overtime because if you thought you'd be  willing to work a three-day trip but if  it turns into a six-day trip  unexpectedly you're going to have a  problem what we want to see is is more  efficient scheduling that goes on  during these irregular operations in a  november 13th memo to flight attendants  southwest airlines said it was adding  more reserve crews to support the  reliability of its scheduling of course  external factors like bad weather or  technical issues play a role in airline  disruptions too but now these issues are  compounded by staffing shortages and  scheduling issues creating chain  reactions that can leave thousands of  passengers stranded you know americans  said it got  knocked off course by some a